    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The fighting began over a Meccan caravan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, but the caravan escaped by another route and the two armies met at Badr anyway.</li><li>The only account of the battle is the Islamic tradition, written down some time later, and some histories doubt how much beyond it can be established.</li><li>Islamic tradition holds that angels took part and that the victory was divine; other sources attribute it to Muhammad's strategy.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Qur'an calls the battle Yawm al-Furqan, 'The Day of the Criterion', and Ibn Ishaq's biography of Muhammad includes a complete name by name roster of the Muslim army.</p></aside>
    </section>

